  "problem_statement": "Literal expressions produce reversed string representation\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen converting literal expressions to their string representation, the output is unexpectedly reversed. This affects grammar string conversion and debugging output.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom parsimonious import Grammar\n\n# Simple literal should display normally\ngrammar = Grammar('foo = \"bar\"')\nprint(str(grammar))\n# Expected: foo = 'bar'\n# Actual: foo = 'rab'\n\n# More complex example with multiple literals\ngrammar = Grammar('rule = \"hello\" \"world\"')\nprint(str(grammar))\n# Expected: rule = 'hello' 'world'  \n# Actual: rule = 'olleh' 'dlrow'\n\n# Parenthesized expressions also affected\ngrammar = Grammar('foo = (\"baz\" \"eggs\")')\nprint(str(grammar))\n# Expected: foo = ('baz' 'eggs')\n# Actual: foo = ('zab' 'sgge')\n```\n\nThe issue appears when any grammar containing literal strings is converted to its string representation - all literal values are displayed in reverse order."
}